%% definitions, direct reads for the branch conditions) and by MEASURING the
%% foreignObject claim rather than repeating it. Same source file, two configs:
%%   default mermaid config  ->  57 <foreignObject>,  0 <text>   (labels invisible in Bitbucket)
%%   assets/mermaid-config.json ->  0 <foreignObject>, 57 <text>  (labels real)
%% Measured on docs/diagrams/process-crew-brief.mmd with mmdc at this anchor. The
%% failure is silent by construction: mmdc exits 0 both times and both SVGs open
%% correctly in a browser tab and a local viewer. Only the <img> embedding path
%% differs, which is exactly the path Markdown uses and the one nobody tests in.
%% --
%% The sentence this diagram was drawn to make visible, and what it bought:
%%   Until manifest version 2, --check verified the INPUTS, not the output. `current`
%%   was  have["hash"] == want and svg.exists()  - the hash covering RENDER_VERSION,
%%   the config fingerprint, the background colour and the normalised source, and
%%   nothing at all about the SVG's CONTENT. A committed SVG that had been truncated,
%%   hand-edited or corrupted after rendering passed --check as long as the file was
%%   still there and the source had not moved. That was this repository's recurring
%%   failure in its CI costume: a green check reporting on the thing it could measure
%%   cheaply, wearing the name of the thing you wanted verified.
%%   MEASURED before the fix rather than argued: a real 15902-byte mmdc render cut in
%%   half mid-attribute, and a zero-byte file, BOTH printed "All 1 diagram(s) up to
%%   date." and exited 0.
%% Now: render records svgHash and --check compares it. A manifest predating version
%%   2 has no hash to compare, so it gets a structural check - non-empty, an <svg
%%   element, a closing tag - and is reported UNVERIFIED rather than counted as up to
%%   date.
%% UNVERIFIED exited 0 until 1.2.2, on the argument that structurally-intact is the
%%   strongest claim available without a recorded hash. It is - and that is the reason
%%   to go red, not a reason to stay green: the caveat was in the output while the exit
%%   code, which is the only part CI reads, still said pass. A green line meaning less
%%   than its reader assumes is the defect this diagram was drawn to expose, so the
%%   fix kept reproducing it one layer down. It now exits 1, naming render --force as
%%   the one-time fix, and stays distinct in the text from DAMAGED: UNVERIFIED does not
%%   claim the SVG is wrong, only that nothing can say it is right.
%%   Both failures are reported in one pass - stale, damaged and unverified sections
%%   print together before the single exit - because returning on the first category
%%   found is how the unverified count used to vanish whenever anything else was wrong.
%%   postprocess()'s foreignObject refusal is still a RENDER-time gate only; it never
%%   runs in CI strategy A, which is the recommended one and needs neither Node nor
%%   Chromium precisely because it does not render.

